  "assessment_summary": "This paper revisits two randomized controlled trials of psychotherapy for depression in India (HAP and THPP) four to five years after treatment, measuring effects on depression, beliefs, economic preferences, and economic outcomes. The study makes several important contributions: documenting long-run persistence of mental health benefits from low-cost psychotherapy, providing novel evidence on how therapy affects belief updating and self-confidence using incentivized experimental methods, and assessing impacts on economic outcomes.\n\nStrengths: (1) The research question is highly policy-relevant. Long-run follow-ups of psychotherapy RCTs in low-income settings are exceedingly rare, and the finding that HAP's effects persist at 5 years with a cost of ~$7 per depression month averted is a significant contribution. (2) The study design is rigorous, building on well-conducted original RCTs with verified randomization. Balance checks at baseline and follow-up are thorough, and attrition is balanced across treatment and control. The 76% follow-up rate after 4-5 years is commendable. (3) The belief-updating experiment is innovative and well-adapted for low-numeracy participants using physical aids (water containers), and the findings on overconfidence and optimistic updating in a depressed population provide genuinely novel evidence against depressive realism. (4) The expert prediction survey provides a useful benchmark for evaluating the novelty and surprise value of the findings. (5) The paper has a pre-analysis plan and reports FDR-adjusted q-values for primary outcomes, with transparent deviations from the PAP documented.\n\nWeaknesses and concerns: (1) Statistical power and precision. The pooled PHQ-9 effect is only marginally significant (p=0.08), and the paper's headline results depend heavily on the HAP trial (N=395 at follow-up). The THPP trial shows essentially null effects, which the authors attribute to high spontaneous remission rates and lower baseline severity. While this explanation is plausible, it means the paper effectively has one trial driving the mental health results. The confidence intervals around many estimates are wide enough to include economically meaningful effects in both directions. (2) The divergence between survey-based and incentivized preference measures is notable. Effects on patience and altruism indices are driven primarily by self-assessment survey questions rather than incentivized behavioral measures. The authors acknowledge this but the interpretation that therapy changes 'self-perceptions' rather than actual behavior versus the alternative that survey measures capture broader real behavior is hard to adjudicate. This is an important distinction for policy implications. (3) The belief-updating analysis, while innovative, has significant data quality issues. 68% of participants have at least one 'irrational' update, 13% never update at all, and 44% of individual observations are non-updates. While robustness checks are provided, the most restricted sample (Panel E, Table A.7) loses significance. The treatment effects on belief updating are similar across both trials despite only HAP reducing depression, which is intriguing but also puzzling—the authors suggest therapy affects beliefs through channels beyond mental health, but the mechanism remains speculative. (4) The null effects on economic outcomes (employment, consumption, productivity) are important but the paper sometimes frames these in a way that understates the ambiguity. The sample is 88% female, and the authors invoke constraints on women's work as an explanation, but the null extends to revealed-preference measures of willingness to work from home and productivity in the bracelet task, which should be less constrained by social norms. The absence of economic effects despite sustained mental health improvements raises questions about the causal pathway from depression to economic outcomes that the paper does not fully resolve. (5) The mediation analysis (Figure A.6) is suggestive but relies on assumptions (sequential ignorability) that are not testable in this design. The finding that behavioral activation mediates effects on preferences is interesting but should be interpreted cautiously. (6) The beliefs about treatment efficacy module was added after data collection began and is not in the pre-analysis plan. While interesting, the finding that both treatment groups become more optimistic about therapy efficacy—even for the ineffective THPP intervention—somewhat undermines the policy implication that experience increases demand for effective treatment. (7) The paper pools two quite different trials (different populations, depression severity thresholds, intervention designs) in its main specification. While trial fixed effects are included, the populations differ substantially (pregnant women with moderate depression vs. general adults with moderately-severe depression), making pooled estimates less interpretable. The paper does present separate results, but the emphasis on pooled estimates in the abstract and introduction can be misleading.\n\nOverall, this is a well-executed and ambitious study that provides valuable evidence on multiple fronts. The long-run mental health finding for HAP is the most policy-relevant and robustly demonstrated result. The belief-updating analysis is innovative and thought-provoking, though the data quality issues and the disconnect from depression effects complicate interpretation. The null results on economic outcomes are informative for the literature on the poverty-depression nexus. The paper is well-written and transparent about its limitations.",
